{"affected_expression_ids":[],"affected_formula_ids":[],"authoring_authority_record_sha256":"0511d44dae43096bed26d8c761085ca6c56b80b2d2004b8e05bb690e9b2047ab","authority_status":"IMMUTABLE_SOURCE_PRESERVED_DERIVED_READER_CORRECTION_DISCLOSED","category":"overgeneralized_incompleteness_claim","correction_id":"TR034-SAR-007","correction_status":"PASS_EXACT_SOURCE_COORDINATE_DISCLOSED_AND_READER_PROJECTION_BOUND","exact_source_lines":{"203":"In 1931, G\\\"odel proved the two ``incompleteness theorems,'' which","204":"showed that this program could not succeed. There is no axiom system","205":"for mathematics which is complete, specifically, the sentence that","206":"expresses the consistency of the axioms is a sentence which can","207":"neither be proved nor refuted."},"exact_source_lines_sha256":"feffeaf559d6d835c6debe4b7a304b9965bd15f7682678b55068f9a38c682723","file":"content/incompleteness/introduction/historical-background.tex","immutable_source_preserved":true,"lines":[203,204,205,206,207],"rationale":"The source says no axiom system for mathematics is complete and identifies the consistency statement as neither provable nor refutable. The precise results require effectiveness, consistency, and strength hypotheses, and unprovability of the formal consistency statement does not by itself establish that its negation is unprovable.","reader_application_kind":"EXACT_SOURCE_ANCHORED_PROSE_REPAIR_PLUS_DISCLOSURE","reader_projection":"Godel showed that Hilbert's program could not succeed: no consistent, effectively axiomatized system strong enough for the intended mathematics is complete. A Godel sentence is undecidable under the first theorem's hypotheses, while the second theorem says that a suitably strong consistent system cannot prove its own formal consistency statement.","reader_projection_disclosed":true,"record_sha256":"356e3f51a85be633da7c8d231fa95dec4940bf9c130ad824af1390b60d1d7f51","source_bytes_status":"IMMUTABLE_AND_UNCHANGED","source_file_sha256":"9bd497cd